  ('ioe-ep1', 118, 'GoldenSloth_Animu', 'av-goldensloth', 'Newbie', 6, true, 'stop calling them "the girl squad" or "the side girls" or "the rival girls." Council 1 is LED by women (Miyuki, Echo, Likko hold 3 of the 5 named roles including President & VP). KO HQ''s most competent person on screen is a woman (Nova). the squad''s strategic brain is a woman (Lisa). the squad''s crisis manager is a woman (Sarah). the YAMAGA fight ends because of a woman (Abigail). this episode is structurally female-led at every layer of staging. if u missed it, that''s on the reader. Eden didn''t hide it.', '2013-10-06T18:28:00Z', 'JST', null, null, false, 'archived'),
  ('ioe-ep1', 119, 'PinkLemon_AMV', 'av-nekotoast', 'Member', 621, false, 'miyuki kagemitsu has only been on screen for 4 minutes & she already gave the most intimidating speech of the season. ''restrictions behind history are merely an excuse to refuse living!'' saori hayami delivered that line w/ pure authority <3', '2013-10-06T18:41:00Z', 'JST', '2013-10-06T18:59:00Z', 'fixed character name', false, 'archived'),
  ('ioe-ep1', 120, 'Nova_FRM_Stan', 'av-guestink', 'Newbie', 228, false, 'compare how Burst draws the girls vs how a generic 2013 action anime would have drawn them Abigail''s finisher would have had a low-angle skirt shot during the air rotation Miyuki''s speech would have had a low-up shot to make her "intimidating" Nova''s tired sigh would have had a chest-pant hold Echo''s parkour would have had impossible mid-air spins Burst drew the EXACT OPPOSITE of all of that, deliberately the camera respects every woman in the episode even when they''re in motion that''s the Takuya filter applied at the storyboard level', '2013-10-06T18:54:00Z', 'JST', null, null, false, 'archived'),
  ('ioe-ep1', 121, 'MoshiMoshi_2007', 'av-cyberlunchbox', 'Newbie', 760, false, 'Nova''s animation is sneaky-incredible. she walks in carrying coffee. that''s the setup. and Burst animated her SHOULDERS dropping by like 1-2 pixels when she looks at the player counter ticking down. they drew exhaustion into her POSTURE, not her dialogue. then Yui Ishikawa delivers "It''s over" flat and Burst doesn''t even cut to a reaction. they trust the audience. that''s Takuya''s whole approach to writing women: emotional weight in the body, not in the lines.', '2013-10-06T19:05:00Z', 'JST', null, null, false, 'archived'),
